const e={id:"int-node-express-questions",title:{en:"Top 25 Node.js & Express Interview Questions & Model Answers",hi:"Top 25 Node.js & Express Interview Questions & Model Answers"},difficulty:"Intermediate",readTime:16,sections:[{type:"concept",heading:{en:"1. The Node.js & Express Architectural Mental Model",hi:"1. Node.js & Express Ka Architectural Mental Model: Interviewer Perspective"},body:{en:`In backend technical interviews for Node.js and Express, interviewers test your understanding of **how JavaScript runs outside the browser**, how asynchronous concurrency works on a **single-threaded event loop**, and how Express handles HTTP requests through a chain of middleware functions.

### The 4 Core Backend Interview Pillars:
1. **The Libuv Architecture & Event Loop Phases**: Understanding that Node.js is single-threaded for JS execution, but multi-threaded under the hood via libuv for I/O operations (file system, DNS, crypto).
2. **Streams, Buffers & Memory Efficiency**: Handling high-throughput payloads without exhausting process RAM via backpressure and streaming chunks.
3. **Express Middleware Pipeline & Error Handling**: Request-response lifecycle, \`next()\` execution order, error middleware signature \`(err, req, res, next)\`, and asynchronous unhandled rejections.
4. **Production Scalability & Process Management**: Clustering across multi-core CPUs, PM2 process management, graceful shutdowns, and memory leak profiling.`,hi:`Backend technical interviews mein interviewers ye dekhte hain ki aapko Node.js ka internal architecture kitna deeply samajh aata hai:

### 4 Mukhya Pillars:
1. **Libuv aur Event Loop Ke Phases**: JavaScript execution single-threaded hai, lekin file I/O, crypto aur DNS resolution ke liye libuv ka thread pool background mein multi-threaded kaam karta hai.
2. **Streams aur Buffers**: Heavy files aur API payloads ko bina RAM exhaust kiye chunk-by-chunk stream karna aur backpressure manage karna.
3. **Express Middleware Chain**: Request se response tak ka lifecycle, \`next()\` call sequence, aur 4-parameter wala centralized error handler \`(err, req, res, next)\`.
4. **Clustering aur Scaling**: CPU ke multiple cores use karne ke liye Cluster module aur PM2 process manager ka upyog.`}},{type:"concept",heading:{en:"2. The 6 Phases of the Node.js Event Loop",hi:"2. Node.js Event Loop Ke 6 Phases Ka Deep Dive"},body:{en:`The libuv event loop continuously cycles through 6 distinct phases in strict order:

\`\`\`
   ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐
┌─>│ 1. TIMERS (setTimeout, setInterval)           │
│  └──────────────────────┬────────────────────────┘
│  ┌──────────────────────▼────────────────────────┐
│  │ 2. PENDING CALLBACKS (I/O system errors)      │
│  └──────────────────────┬────────────────────────┘
│  ┌──────────────────────▼────────────────────────┐
│  │ 3. IDLE, PREPARE (internal libuv operations)  │
│  └──────────────────────┬────────────────────────┘
│  ┌──────────────────────▼────────────────────────┐
│  │ 4. POLL (retrieve new I/O events, sockets)    │
│  └──────────────────────┬────────────────────────┘
│  ┌──────────────────────▼────────────────────────┐
│  │ 5. CHECK (setImmediate callbacks execute)     │
│  └──────────────────────┬────────────────────────┘
│  ┌──────────────────────▼────────────────────────┐
│  │ 6. CLOSE CALLBACKS (e.g. socket.on('close'))  │
└──┴───────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   * Microtasks (process.nextTick & Promises) drain
     BETWEEN EVERY SINGLE PHASE transition!
\`\`\`

### Microtask Queues:
- **process.nextTick()**: Highest priority! Executes immediately after the current synchronous operation finishes, before the event loop advances to ANY next phase.
- **Promise Microtasks**: Executes immediately after \`process.nextTick\` drains.`,hi:"Node.js ka Event Loop in 6 phases mein ghoomta hai:\n\n1. **Timers**: `setTimeout` aur `setInterval` ke expired callbacks execute hote hain.\n2. **Pending Callbacks**: TCP errors ya deferred system operations run hote hain.\n3. **Idle / Prepare**: Node.js ke internal libuv maintenance operations.\n4. **Poll Phase**: Sabse crucial phase jahan naye I/O events (network connections, database queries) sune jaate hain.\n5. **Check Phase**: `setImmediate()` ke callbacks execute hote hain.\n6. **Close Callbacks**: Closed connections (jaise `socket.on('close')`) cleanup hote hain.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **process.nextTick() aur Promise Queues**: Har phase ke transition ke beech mein Microtask queue poori tarah drain (empty) hoti hai! `process.nextTick` Promise se bhi pehle run hota hai."}},{type:"analogy",heading:{en:"Real-Life Analogy: The Single Indian Post Office Token Clerk",hi:"Real-Life Analogy: Indian Post Office Ka Single Token Clerk (Event Loop)"},body:{en:`Imagine a busy Indian General Post Office (GPO) in New Delhi:

- **The Traditional Multi-Threaded Model (Apache / Java Tomcat)**:
  For every customer entering the building, the post office hires a brand new full-time postal clerk. If 1,000 customers enter, 1,000 clerks sit at 1,000 chairs. If 950 customers are waiting for speed post parcels to arrive from Mumbai (waiting for I/O), 950 clerks sit idle while company payroll and office chair space (RAM and CPU thread stack memory) run dry!
- **The Node.js Event Loop Model (The Single Master Clerk)**:
  Only **one master clerk** (the single JavaScript main thread) stands at the primary counter:
  1. Customer A arrives requesting passport verification (Heavy I/O). The master clerk gives Customer A a token number and passes the paperwork through a back-office window to the **4 back-office assistants (Libuv Thread Pool)**.
  2. The master clerk does not sit idle! He immediately serves Customer B at the counter (Non-blocking I/O).
  3. When an assistant finishes Customer A's verification, they place the completed stamped passport into the **Poll Queue**.
  4. As soon as the master clerk is between counter requests, he picks up Customer A's token, calls out their number, and hands over the passport (**Callback execution**).`,hi:`New Delhi ke busy Post Office ka example dekhein:

- **Purana Multi-Threaded Model (Java/Apache)**:
  Har naye customer ke liye ek naya clerk hire hota hai. Agar 1000 customers aaye toh 1000 chairs aur 1000 clerks chahiye. Agar customers parcel aane ka intezar kar rahe hain, toh clerks haath par haath rakh kar baithe rehte hain (heavy RAM aur thread context switching).
- **Node.js Model (Single Smart Clerk)**:
  Counter par sirf **ek hi master clerk** (Single JS Thread) baithta hai:
  1. Customer A aakar bolta hai: *"Mera heavy background verification karo"*. Master clerk use token deta hai aur file peeche baithe **4 assistants (Libuv Thread Pool)** ko de deta hai.
  2. Master clerk wait nahi karta! Wo turant agle customer B ko ticket bechne lagta hai (Non-blocking).
  3. Jab background assistant Customer A ki file stamp kar leta hai, toh wo use **Poll Queue** mein daal deta hai.
  4. Master clerk jaise hi counter par free hota hai, Customer A ka token call karke file deliver kar deta hai.`}},{type:"syntax",heading:{en:"Event Loop Execution Order & Stream Pipelines",hi:"Event Loop Priority Execution Aur Streams Pipeline Syntax"},body:{en:`### 1. Execution Order of Asynchronous Primitives:
\`\`\`javascript
// index.js - Predict the exact output order:
console.log('1. Synchronous script start');

setTimeout(() => {
  console.log('2. setTimeout timer (Macrotask - Timers Phase)');
}, 0);

setImmediate(() => {
  console.log('3. setImmediate (Macrotask - Check Phase)');
});

Promise.resolve().then(() => {
  console.log('4. Promise microtask (Microtask Queue)');
});

process.nextTick(() => {
  console.log('5. process.nextTick (High-priority Microtask)');
});

console.log('6. Synchronous script end');

// OUTPUT:
// 1. Synchronous script start
// 6. Synchronous script end
// 5. process.nextTick (High-priority Microtask)
// 4. Promise microtask (Microtask Queue)
// 2. setTimeout timer OR 3. setImmediate (depends on process tick, setImmediate wins inside I/O callbacks)
\`\`\`

### 2. Streams with Backpressure Safe Pipeline:
\`\`\`javascript
import fs from 'fs';
import zlib from 'zlib';
import { pipeline } from 'stream/promises';

// Memory-safe streaming of multi-gigabyte files with gzip compression
async function compressFile(sourcePath, destPath) {
  try {
    await pipeline(
      fs.createReadStream(sourcePath),  // Readable stream (chunks)
      zlib.createGzip(),                // Transform stream (compress)
      fs.createWriteStream(destPath)    // Writable stream (disk)
    );
    console.log('Compression complete with zero memory spikes!');
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Pipeline failed:', err);
  }
}
\`\`\``,hi:`### Asynchronous Primitives Ka Priority Order:
\`\`\`javascript
// Priority hierarchy:
// 1. Synchronous Call Stack (Sabse pehle)
// 2. process.nextTick() (Microtask queue ka raja)
// 3. Promise.then() (Microtask queue)
// 4. Event loop phases: Timers -> Poll -> Check (setImmediate)
\`\`\`

### Backpressure-Safe Stream Pipeline:
Bina memory spike ke 5GB ki file compress karne ka standard tareeqa \`pipeline\` utility use karna hai jo automatic memory backpressure aur cleanup handle karti hai.`}},{type:"example",heading:{en:"Example 1: Express Centralized Error Handling & Async Wrapper",hi:"Example 1: Express Mein Centralized Error Handling & Async Controller Wrapper"},body:{en:`In Express 4, unhandled promise rejections in \`async\` route handlers crash the Node process unless passed to \`next(err)\`. Here is the industry-standard clean solution:

\`\`\`javascript
// utils/asyncHandler.js
// Higher-order function eliminating repetitive try/catch blocks
export const asyncHandler = (fn) => (req, res, next) => {
  Promise.resolve(fn(req, res, next)).catch(next);
};

// middleware/errorMiddleware.js
// Custom operational error class
export class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, statusCode) {
    super(message);
    this.statusCode = statusCode;
    this.isOperational = true;
    Error.captureStackTrace(this, this.constructor);
  }
}

// Global 4-parameter error handler
export const errorHandler = (err, req, res, next) => {
  const statusCode = err.statusCode || 500;
  const message = err.isOperational ? err.message : 'Internal Server Error';

  console.error('[Error Pipeline]:', {
    message: err.message,
    stack: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production' ? null : err.stack,
    url: req.originalUrl,
  });

  res.status(statusCode).json({
    success: false,
    statusCode,
    message,
    ...(process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development' && { stack: err.stack }),
  });
};

// controllers/userController.js
export const getUserProfile = asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {
  const user = await User.findById(req.params.id);
  if (!user) {
    throw new AppError('User profile not found', 404);
  }
  res.json({ success: true, user });
});
\`\`\``,hi:"Express 4 mein agar async function mein error throw ho aur `next(err)` na ho, toh server hang ho sakta hai. Production apps mein `asyncHandler` aur centralized 4-parameter error middleware use kiya jata hai:\n\n```javascript\n// 4-parameter error handler Express ka standard hai:\napp.use((err, req, res, next) => {\n  const status = err.statusCode || 500;\n  res.status(status).json({ success: false, message: err.message });\n});\n```"}},{type:"example",heading:{en:"Example 2: Memory Leak Detection via Heap Profiling & Event Listener Cleanup",hi:"Example 2: Node.js Memory Leaks Detect Karna Aur Unhe Fix Karna"},body:{en:`A classic interview question: *"How do memory leaks occur in Node.js, and how do you debug and fix them?"*

### Common Cause: Dangling Global Event Listeners & Closures
\`\`\`javascript
import { EventEmitter } from 'events';
const globalEmitter = new EventEmitter();

// ❌ MEMORY LEAK: Subscribing without unsubscribing
export function handleUserSession(socket) {
  const bigDataBuffer = Buffer.alloc(10 * 1024 * 1024); // 10MB per user

  // Listener closure keeps bigDataBuffer alive in RAM forever!
  globalEmitter.on('broadcast', () => {
    socket.emit('message', bigDataBuffer);
  });
  // When socket disconnects, listener remains registered in globalEmitter!
}

// ✅ FIX: Clean up listeners on socket disconnect
export function handleUserSessionFixed(socket) {
  const bigDataBuffer = Buffer.alloc(10 * 1024 * 1024);

  const onBroadcast = () => {
    socket.emit('message', bigDataBuffer);
  };

  globalEmitter.on('broadcast', onBroadcast);

  socket.on('disconnect', () => {
    // Explicitly remove listener to allow garbage collector to free bigDataBuffer
    globalEmitter.off('broadcast', onBroadcast);
  });
}
\`\`\``,hi:"Node.js mein memory leaks tab hote hain jab Garbage Collector (V8) unreferenced memory ko free nahi kar pata kyunki koi global reference ya event listener use pakad ke baitha hai:\n\n- **Khatarnak**: Global `EventEmitter.on()` lagana aur client disconnect hone par use `off()` na karna.\n- **Solution**: Disconnect ya cleanup par hamesha `removeListener` ya `off` call karein taaki V8 us memory ko free kar sake."}},{type:"example",heading:{en:"Example 3: Multi-Core Clustering & Zero-Downtime Reloads",hi:"Example 3: Multi-Core CPU Par Cluster Module Se Scale Karna"},body:{en:`Node.js runs on a single CPU core by default. On an 8-core server, 7 cores remain completely idle unless you cluster worker processes:

\`\`\`javascript
// server.js
import cluster from 'cluster';
import os from 'os';
import http from 'http';
import express from 'express';

const numCPUs = os.cpus().length;

if (cluster.isPrimary) {
  console.log(\`Primary process \${process.pid} is orchestrating \${numCPUs} worker processes\`);

  // Fork a worker process for every CPU core
  for (let i = 0; i < numCPUs; i++) {
    cluster.fork();
  }

  // Auto-restart dead worker processes (Self-Healing architecture)
  cluster.on('exit', (worker, code, signal) => {
    console.warn(\`Worker \${worker.process.pid} died (exit code: \${code}). Spawning replacement worker...\`);
    cluster.fork();
  });
} else {
  // Workers share the same underlying TCP port 5000 via OS socket multiplexing
  const app = express();
  app.get('/api/health', (req, res) => {
    res.json({ status: 'ok', workerPid: process.pid });
  });

  app.listen(5000, () => {
    console.log(\`Worker \${process.pid} listening on port 5000\`);
  });
}
\`\`\``,hi:"Node.js by default sirf 1 CPU core use karta hai. 8-core CPU wale server par baaki 7 cores bekar rehte hain. `cluster` module use karke primary process har CPU core ke liye ek worker fork karta hai. Sabhi workers same port (jaise 5000) share karte hain."}},{type:"mistakes",heading:{en:"Common Node.js & Express Mistakes Candidates Make",hi:"Node.js & Express Interviews Mein Candidates Ki 3 Sabse Badi Galatiyan"},body:{en:"### 1. Blocking the Event Loop with Synchronous Operations\n- **Mistake**: Using `fs.readFileSync()`, `crypto.pbkdf2Sync()`, or heavy JSON parsing of a 50MB string inside a request handler.\n- **Consequence**: The single thread halts completely; all other 5,000 active client connections freeze and time out!\n- **Fix**: Always use asynchronous promises (`fs.promises.readFile`) or offload heavy CPU work to a `WorkerThread` or message queue (BullMQ).\n\n### 2. Forgetting to Call `return` Before `res.json()`\n- **Mistake**: Calling `res.status(400).json(...)` inside an `if` check without `return`.\n- **Consequence**: Execution continues below, triggering the infamous `Error [ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT]: Cannot set headers after they are sent to the client`.\n- **Fix**: Always `return res.status(400).json(...);`.\n\n### 3. Reading Entire Files into RAM with `fs.readFile`\n- **Mistake**: Reading a 2GB video or PDF file into a buffer via `fs.readFile` to serve it to the client.\n- **Consequence**: V8 max heap memory exhaustion (`FATAL ERROR: Ineffective mark-compacts near heap limit Allocation failed - JavaScript heap out of memory`).\n- **Fix**: Pipe via stream: `fs.createReadStream(path).pipe(res)`.",hi:"### 1. Synchronous Code Se Event Loop Block Karna:\n- `fs.readFileSync` ya heavy CPU calculation request handler mein chalana. Isse poora server baaki saare users ke liye freeze ho jata hai.\n\n### 2. Headers Already Sent Error:\n- `res.status(400).json()` likhte waqt `return` bhool jaana. Code aage badhta hai aur second response bhejne ki koshish karta hai, jisse crash hota hai.\n\n### 3. Poori Heavy File RAM Mein Load Karna:\n- 1GB ki file `fs.readFile` se memory mein load karna. Node heap crash ho jata hai. Hamesha `createReadStream().pipe(res)` use karein."}},{type:"best-practices",heading:{en:"Production Architecture Best Practices for Node.js",hi:"Production Node.js Applications Ki Top Best Practices"},body:{en:"1. **Graceful Shutdown**: Intercept `SIGTERM` and `SIGINT` signals to stop accepting new HTTP connections, finish ongoing database transactions, and close MongoDB/Redis pools cleanly before exiting.\n2. **Security Headers**: Mount `helmet()` middleware to protect against clickjacking, XSS, MIME sniffing, and enforce HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS).\n3. **Validate Environment Variables at Startup**: Use Zod or Joi to validate `process.env` variables on server boot; fail fast if `JWT_SECRET` or `MONGO_URI` is missing.\n4. **Use Structured Logging**: Replace `console.log` with Winston or Pino to output structured JSON logs with correlation IDs for distributed tracing.\n5. **Reverse Proxy Configuration**: Always configure `app.set('trust proxy', 1)` when behind Nginx, AWS ALB, Cloudflare, or Render so client IPs are parsed accurately.",hi:"1. **Graceful Shutdown**: Server band hone par `SIGTERM` intercept karein taaki chalti hui DB queries complete ho sakein.\n2. **Helmet Middleware**: Express mein security headers automatically set karne ke liye `app.use(helmet())` lagayein.\n3. **Environment Validation**: Server start hone se pehle Zod ya Joi se check karein ki `MONGO_URI` aur `JWT_SECRET` maujood hain ya nahi.\n4. **Pino / Winston**: Production mein `console.log` ke badle structured JSON logging use karein.\n5. **Trust Proxy**: Nginx ya Cloudflare ke peeche hone par `app.set('trust proxy', 1)` zaroor karein."}},{type:"interview",heading:{en:"Top 25 Node.js & Express Interview Questions Bank",hi:"Top 25 Node.js & Express Interview Questions Bank (Comprehensive Q&A)"},body:{en:"### Q1: Is Node.js truly single-threaded?\n**Answer**: Node.js executes JavaScript on a **single main thread** (one Call Stack and one Memory Heap). However, Node.js uses **libuv**, which maintains a multi-threaded worker pool (default: 4 threads, configurable via `UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE`). System operations such as asynchronous file I/O (`fs`), cryptographic hashing (`crypto.pbkdf2`), zlib compression, and DNS lookups (`dns.lookup`) are offloaded to these background C++ worker threads.\n\n---\n\n### Q2: What is the difference between `process.nextTick()` and `setImmediate()`?\n**Answer**:\n- `process.nextTick()` is **not** part of the event loop. It executes immediately after the current operation finishes on the call stack, before the event loop advances to the next phase.\n- `setImmediate()` is designed to execute in the **Check phase** of the event loop, immediately after the I/O Poll phase.\n- Rule of thumb: If you need to allow I/O to be processed first, use `setImmediate()`. To defer execution until after current stack without waiting for the next tick of the loop, use `process.nextTick()`.\n\n---\n\n### Q3: How do Streams differ from Buffers, and what is backpressure?\n**Answer**:\n- A **Buffer** is a fixed-size chunk of raw binary memory allocated outside the V8 heap. It holds entire datasets in RAM at once.\n- A **Stream** is an abstraction for reading or writing data sequentially in chunks over time, consuming minimal memory.\n- **Backpressure** occurs when the *Readable stream* produces data faster than the *Writable stream* can write it to the destination (e.g. disk or network socket). Node.js handles backpressure automatically when you use `readable.pipe(writable)` or `stream.pipeline()`, pausing the readable stream until the writable buffer drains.\n\n---\n\n### Q4: How does Express middleware work internally?\n**Answer**: Express maintains an internal routing stack array. Each `app.use()` or route handler pushes a layer containing a path regex and a callback function. When an incoming HTTP request arrives, Express invokes the first matched layer and passes `(req, res, next)`. Calling `next()` advances the pointer to the next matching layer in the stack. If `next(err)` is called with an argument, Express skips all standard middleware and jumps straight to the next registered error-handling middleware (functions with exactly 4 arguments: `(err, req, res, next)`).\n\n---\n\n### Q5: What is the difference between `cluster` module and `worker_threads`?\n**Answer**:\n- `cluster`: Spawns multiple independent OS processes (forks). Each worker process has its own isolated memory space, its own V8 instance, and its own event loop. They communicate via IPC (Inter-Process Communication) and share the same server TCP port.\n- `worker_threads`: Runs multiple threads within the **same single OS process**. Worker threads share memory (via `SharedArrayBuffer`) and are lightweight, ideal for CPU-heavy tasks (e.g. image manipulation, encryption) rather than I/O-bound web server scaling.\n\n---\n\n### Q6: How do you gracefully shut down a Node.js Express server?\n**Answer**:\n```javascript\nconst server = app.listen(PORT);\n\nconst gracefulShutdown = (signal) => {\n  console.log(`Received ${signal}. Shutting down gracefully...`);\n  server.close(async () => {\n    console.log('Closed all incoming HTTP connections.');\n    await mongoose.connection.close();\n    await redisClient.quit();\n    process.exit(0);\n  });\n\n  // Force shutdown after 10s timeout in case connections remain stuck\n  setTimeout(() => {\n    console.error('Could not close connections in time, forcefully shutting down');\n    process.exit(1);\n  }, 10000);\n};\n\nprocess.on('SIGTERM', () => gracefulShutdown('SIGTERM'));\nprocess.on('SIGINT', () => gracefulShutdown('SIGINT'));\n```\n\n---\n\n### Q7: Why should you avoid using `body-parser` or `express.json()` with huge payload limits?\n**Answer**: If you set `express.json({ limit: '50mb' })`, an attacker can send thousands of concurrent 50MB JSON payloads, causing the server to parse huge strings into V8 memory simultaneously, leading to DoS (Denial of Service) via heap exhaustion. Standard APIs should restrict JSON limits to `100kb` - `1mb`, and use direct multipart streaming (Multer / Busboy) for large media uploads.\n\n---\n\n### Q8: What are Unhandled Rejections and Uncaught Exceptions?\n**Answer**:\n- **Uncaught Exception**: A synchronous error that was never caught in a `try/catch` block. It leaves the process in an undefined, corrupted state. The process must log the error and terminate (`process.exit(1)`).\n- **Unhandled Rejection**: A Promise that rejected without a `.catch()` handler. In modern Node.js (v15+), unhandled promise rejections terminate the process with a non-zero exit code by default.\n\n---\n\n### Q9: What is the purpose of the `NODE_ENV` environment variable?\n**Answer**: When `NODE_ENV=production`:\n1. Express enables aggressive template caching and disables verbose HTML error stack formatting.\n2. Many libraries (like Mongoose, React, Sequelize) disable diagnostic assertions and logging, increasing throughput by up to 3x.\n3. Debugging tools and sourcemaps are stripped.\n\n---\n\n### Q10: How do you prevent SQL Injection / NoSQL Injection in Express?\n**Answer**:\n- For NoSQL (MongoDB): Sanitize input using `express-mongo-sanitize` to strip out `$` and `.` characters from `req.body` and `req.query`, preventing operators like `{ username: { $gt: \"\" } }`.\n- Always validate incoming payload schemas strictly using Zod or Joi.\n- Never construct queries via string concatenation. Use parameterized queries or Mongoose schema casting.\n\n---\n\n### Q11: What is the difference between `res.send()`, `res.json()`, and `res.end()`?\n**Answer**:\n- `res.end()`: Low-level Node.js method to close the HTTP response stream immediately without setting Content-Type.\n- `res.send()`: High-level Express method that inspects the data type (string, Buffer, array, object), sets appropriate `Content-Type` (e.g. `text/html`), calculates `Content-Length`, and handles ETag caching headers.\n- `res.json()`: Explicitly formats the data as JSON, runs `JSON.stringify()`, and sets `Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8`.\n\n---\n\n### Q12: How does V8 Garbage Collection work in Node.js?\n**Answer**: V8 uses a generational garbage collection strategy:\n- **Young Generation (Scavenger)**: Short-lived objects are allocated here. Fast and frequent minor GC cycles copy living objects between \"From\" and \"To\" semi-spaces.\n- **Old Generation (Mark-Sweep & Mark-Compact)**: Objects that survive multiple young GC cycles are promoted here. A major GC cycle runs periodically to mark alive objects, sweep dead ones, and compact fragmented memory.\n\n---\n\n### Q13: What is Event Emitter and what is the default maximum listeners limit?\n**Answer**: The `EventEmitter` class is Node's built-in pub-sub pattern implementation. By default, an emitter allows up to **10 listeners** per event. If more than 10 are added, Node prints a warning (`MaxListenersExceededWarning`) to alert developers to possible memory leaks. The limit can be adjusted using `emitter.setMaxListeners(n)`.\n\n---\n\n### Q14: How does Libuv manage the thread pool size?\n**Answer**: Libuv defaults to 4 worker threads. On high-core servers doing intensive cryptographic hashing (e.g., bcrypt password hashing) or image manipulation, this default 4-thread pool becomes a bottleneck. It can be scaled by setting the environment variable `UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE=16` before the Node.js runtime initializes.\n\n---\n\n### Q15: How does CORS work, and what is a preflight request?\n**Answer**: Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) is a browser security mechanism. When a frontend at `https://app.com` makes a non-simple HTTP request (e.g., `PUT`, `DELETE`, or with custom headers like `Authorization`) to `https://api.com`, the browser first sends an automated `OPTIONS` HTTP request (the **preflight request**). The server must respond with headers (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin`, `Access-Control-Allow-Methods`, `Access-Control-Allow-Headers`) to authorize the actual request.\n\n---\n\n### Q16: What is the difference between `npm install --save` and `npm install --save-dev`?\n**Answer**:\n- `dependencies` (`--save`): Packages required at runtime in production (e.g., `express`, `mongoose`, `bcrypt`).\n- `devDependencies` (`--save-dev`): Packages required only during local development and testing (e.g., `jest`, `nodemon`, `supertest`). In production deployment, running `npm ci --omit=dev` skips dev dependencies, saving build time and container image size.\n\n---\n\n### Q17: What is the difference between `require()` (CommonJS) and `import` (ES Modules)?\n**Answer**:\n- CommonJS (`require`): Synchronous, evaluated at runtime, dynamic loading permitted inside `if` conditions. Default in older Node.\n- ES Modules (`import`): Asynchronous, statically analyzed at compile time, tree-shakeable, top-level `await` supported natively. Enabled in Node.js via `\"type\": \"module\"` in `package.json`.\n\n---\n\n### Q18: What is the role of `helmet` in Express?\n**Answer**: `helmet` is a collection of 15 smaller middleware functions that secure Express apps by setting various HTTP headers:\n- `Content-Security-Policy` (restricts trusted script/style origins)\n- `X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN` (mitigates clickjacking)\n- `Strict-Transport-Security` (forces HTTPS connections)\n- `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` (prevents MIME sniffing)\n- Hides the revealing `X-Powered-By: Express` header.\n\n---\n\n### Q19: How do you handle file uploads in Express safely?\n**Answer**:\n1. Use **Multer** or **Busboy** to stream incoming `multipart/form-data` without holding entire files in RAM.\n2. Validate MIME types and file extensions strictly (do not trust client-supplied headers; verify magic bytes).\n3. Enforce maximum file size limits (e.g., 5MB).\n4. Stream files directly to cloud storage (AWS S3 or Cloudinary) rather than saving to local server disk storage.\n\n---\n\n### Q20: What is PM2 and why is it used in production?\n**Answer**: PM2 is an enterprise-grade Production Process Manager for Node.js. It provides:\n1. **Cluster Mode**: Spawns workers across all CPU cores with automatic load balancing without changing any code.\n2. **Auto-Restart**: Instantly restarts crashed processes.\n3. **Zero-Downtime Reloads**: Reloads application code sequentially worker-by-worker (`pm2 reload all`).\n4. **Log Management**: Log rotation and metrics monitoring.\n\n---\n\n### Q21: What is the difference between `spawn()` and `fork()` in the `child_process` module?\n**Answer**:\n- `spawn()`: Launches a new external process with an arbitrary command (e.g. `spawn('ls', ['-lh'])`). Returns streams for `stdin`, `stdout`, `stderr`. Memory-efficient for streaming large data.\n- `fork()`: A special case of `spawn()` dedicated specifically to spawning new Node.js instances. Establishes a built-in IPC (Inter-Process Communication) messaging channel between parent and child via `process.send()`.\n\n---\n\n### Q22: What causes `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded` and how do you resolve it?\n**Answer**: This error occurs when a function calls itself recursively without an adequate base terminating condition, exceeding the maximum call stack frames allowed by V8 (typically ~10,000 frames). It can be resolved by:\n1. Adding an explicit base termination check.\n2. Converting the recursion to an iterative `while` / `for` loop.\n3. Trampolining or deferring calls via `queueMicrotask()` or `setImmediate()`.\n\n---\n\n### Q23: How do you implement rate limiting in Express?\n**Answer**: Use `express-rate-limit` backed by a Redis store (`rate-limit-redis`) for distributed horizontal scaling. The middleware tracks IP requests in a rolling time window and responds with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) when limits are exceeded.\n\n---\n\n### Q24: What is the difference between Authentication and Authorization?\n**Answer**:\n- **Authentication (AuthN)**: Verifying *who* the user is (e.g., checking credentials via email/password or JWT signature).\n- **Authorization (AuthZ)**: Verifying *what* permissions the authenticated user possesses (e.g., checking if the user role is `admin` or `editor` before granting access to a resource).\n\n---\n\n### Q25: How do you debug memory leaks in production Node.js?\n**Answer**:\n1. Take two **V8 Heap Snapshots** 15 minutes apart using Chrome DevTools via `--inspect` or the `v8` built-in module.\n2. Compare the two snapshots in the \"Comparison\" view.\n3. Look for classes with a continuously growing **Delta** count (especially `Closure`, `Array`, `Object`, and `EventEmitter`).\n4. Inspect the **Retainers tree** to identify the root reference preventing garbage collection.",hi:"### Top 25 Node & Express Q&A:\nEvent loop phases, streams vs buffers, backpressure, `process.nextTick` vs `setImmediate`, libuv thread pool, error middleware `(err, req, res, next)`, PM2 clustering, memory leak debugging, aur security headers sabhi cover kiye gaye hain."}},{type:"summary",heading:{en:"Quick Summary & Interview Cheat Sheet",hi:"Quick Summary & Interview Revision Notes"},body:{en:"- **Event Loop Execution**: Synchronous Code -> `process.nextTick` -> Promises -> Timers -> Poll -> Check (`setImmediate`).\n- **Libuv Worker Threads**: 4 threads handling file I/O, DNS, and crypto; scales via `UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE`.\n- **Streams**: Essential for large datasets to avoid memory exhaustion; always use `stream.pipeline()` for automatic backpressure.\n- **Express Error Handling**: Always require 4 parameters `(err, req, res, next)` in global error handlers.\n- **Clustering**: Scale single-threaded Node.js across all server CPU cores using the Cluster module or PM2.",hi:"- **Event Loop**: Synchronous Code -> `process.nextTick` -> Promises -> Timers -> Poll -> Check (`setImmediate`).\n- **Libuv Threads**: 4 default background threads for heavy file, DNS, aur crypto tasks.\n- **Streams**: Heavy data ke liye `pipeline()` use karein taaki RAM crash na ho.\n- **Express Errors**: Global error middleware mein hamesha 4 parameters `(err, req, res, next)` hone chahiye.\n- **Clustering**: Multi-core CPU ka poora fayda uthane ke liye PM2 ya Cluster module use karein."}}],quiz:[{question:{en:"Which asynchronous queue has the highest priority and executes immediately after the current synchronous operation finishes, before any event loop phase transitions?",hi:"Kaunsa asynchronous callback sabse high priority rakhta hai aur event loop ke kisi bhi phase transition se pehle run hota hai?"},options:[{en:"setImmediate()",hi:"setImmediate()"},{en:"process.nextTick()",hi:"process.nextTick()"},{en:"setTimeout(fn, 0)",hi:"setTimeout(fn, 0)"},{en:"socket.on('data')",hi:"socket.on('data')"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"process.nextTick() is not technically part of the event loop; it runs on the microtask queue with higher priority than Promises, draining immediately after the current call stack clears.",hi:"process.nextTick() sabse high-priority microtask queue par chalta hai aur Promises se bhi pehle execute hota hai."}},{question:{en:"Why is the 4th parameter 'next' required in Express error-handling middleware even if it is not explicitly called?",hi:"Express error-handling middleware mein 4th parameter 'next' likhna kyun compulsory hai chahe use call na karein?"},options:[{en:"Because JavaScript requires all functions to have 4 parameters",hi:"Kyunki JS mein har function mein 4 arguments hote hain"},{en:"Express checks fn.length === 4 at registration time using Function.prototype.length; without 4 declared arguments, Express treats it as a standard route handler rather than an error handler",hi:"Express function.length check karta hai; agar 4 arguments declare na hon toh Express use error handler nahi balki normal middleware manta hai"},{en:"It prevents Node.js from using more than 1MB RAM",hi:"Ye Node ko 1MB se zyada RAM lene se rokkta hai"},{en:"It automatically enables SSL certificates",hi:"Ye automatic SSL enable karta hai"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"Express checks middleware arity using Function.length. A middleware with 3 parameters is treated as normal (req, res, next). Only functions with arity 4 (err, req, res, next) receive intercepted errors.",hi:"Express runtime par fn.length check karta hai. Agar exact 4 arguments na hon, toh wo error intercept nahi kar pata."}},{question:{en:"What problem does 'backpressure' solve in Node.js streams?",hi:"Node.js streams mein 'backpressure' kis samasya ko solve karta hai?"},options:[{en:"It cools down the physical computer hardware",hi:"Ye CPU hardware ko thanda karta hai"},{en:"It prevents a fast Readable stream from overwhelming a slow Writable stream and exhausting process RAM",hi:"Ye tez readable stream ko dheemi writable stream par heavy data flood karne aur RAM bharne se rokkta hai"},{en:"It converts JSON to XML",hi:"Ye JSON ko XML mein convert karta hai"},{en:"It encrypts passwords in bcrypt",hi:"Ye passwords encrypt karta hai"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"Backpressure pauses the readable stream when the writable stream's internal buffer is full, resuming when the buffer drains to ensure constant, low memory consumption.",hi:"Backpressure readable stream ko pause kar deta hai jab tak writable buffer khali na ho, jisse RAM memory crash nahi hoti."}},{question:{en:"What happens if you execute a heavy synchronous computation (like 10 seconds of while loop) in an Express route handler?",hi:"Agar aap Express route handler mein 10 second ka synchronous while loop chala dein toh kya hoga?"},options:[{en:"Node.js will automatically create 10 new threads to handle other users",hi:"Node.js automatically naye threads bana lega"},{en:"The single JavaScript thread blocks completely; all other active user requests freeze and become unresponsive until the loop completes",hi:"Single JS thread poori tarah block ho jayega; baaki saare users ke requests freeze ho jayenge jab tak loop khatam nahi hota"},{en:"Only that specific user experiences latency, other users are unaffected",hi:"Sirf usi user ko delay hoga, baaki unaffected rahenge"},{en:"Express throws an automatic SyntaxError",hi:"Express automatic SyntaxError fekega"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"Because JavaScript execution is single-threaded, any synchronous CPU-intensive block halts the Call Stack, preventing the Event Loop from processing any other incoming or pending requests.",hi:"JavaScript single-threaded hai. Agar ek request Call Stack ko block kar deti hai, toh poora server baaki sabhi users ke liye freeze ho jata hai."}},{question:{en:"What is the default size of the Libuv background thread pool, and how can it be scaled?",hi:"Libuv background thread pool ka default size kitna hota hai aur ise kaise badhaya ja sakta hai?"},options:[{en:"Default is 4 threads; scaled by setting the UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE environment variable before Node startup",hi:"Default 4 threads hote hain; Node start hone se pehle UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE environment variable set karke scale kiya jata hai"},{en:"Default is 1 thread; scaled via npm install threads",hi:"Default 1 thread hota hai; npm install threads se scale hota hai"},{en:"Default is 100 threads; cannot be changed",hi:"Default 100 threads hote hain; change nahi ho sakta"},{en:"Default is 0 threads; Node.js never uses threads",hi:"Default 0 threads hote hain"}],correctAnswer:0,explanation:{en:"Libuv defaults to 4 worker threads for fs, crypto, and dns operations. It can be scaled up to 128 threads via process.env.UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE before initializing the runtime.",hi:"Libuv by default 4 threads use karta hai file aur crypto operations ke liye. UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE se ise 128 tak badhaya ja sakta hai."}},{question:{en:"What is the core architectural difference between Node's cluster module and worker_threads?",hi:"Node.js ke cluster module aur worker_threads mein mukhya architectural farq kya hai?"},options:[{en:"cluster forks separate OS processes with isolated memory spaces sharing a port; worker_threads runs multiple threads within the same single OS process sharing memory",hi:"cluster alag OS processes fork karta hai jinki apni isolated memory hoti hai aur wo port share karte hain; worker_threads ek hi single OS process ke andar multiple threads chalata hai jo memory share karte hain"},{en:"cluster is for frontend React only; worker_threads is for Express",hi:"cluster sirf React ke liye hai"},{en:"worker_threads cannot run JavaScript",hi:"worker_threads JS nahi chala sakte"},{en:"There is zero architectural difference",hi:"Dono mein koi farq nahi hai"}],correctAnswer:0,explanation:{en:"cluster scales across CPU cores by creating independent processes communicating via IPC. worker_threads enables multi-threading within a single process with shared memory for CPU-heavy tasks.",hi:"cluster alag-alag processes banata hai jabki worker_threads ek hi process ke andar lightweight threads chalata hai."}},{question:{en:"Inside an asynchronous I/O callback (such as fs.readFile), which executes first: setImmediate or setTimeout(fn, 0)?",hi:"Kisi async I/O callback (jaise fs.readFile) ke andar pehle kaun execute hoga: setImmediate ya setTimeout(fn, 0)?"},options:[{en:"setTimeout(fn, 0) always executes first",hi:"setTimeout(fn, 0) hamesha pehle chalta hai"},{en:"setImmediate always executes first because the event loop advances directly from the Poll phase to the Check phase",hi:"setImmediate hamesha pehle chalta hai kyunki event loop Poll phase ke theek baad Check phase mein jata hai"},{en:"Neither; both are cancelled",hi:"Dono cancel ho jaate hain"},{en:"Their order is completely random in every OS",hi:"Inka order completely random hota hai"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"I/O callbacks execute in the Poll phase. Immediately after the Poll phase, the event loop enters the Check phase where setImmediate callbacks reside, before looping back to Timers.",hi:"I/O callbacks Poll phase mein chalti hain jiske theek baad Check phase (setImmediate) aata hai, isliye setImmediate hamesha pehle chalta hai."}},{question:{en:"What is the critical sequence of actions required during a graceful shutdown of an Express server upon receiving SIGTERM?",hi:"SIGTERM signal aane par Express server ke graceful shutdown ka sahi sequence kya hai?"},options:[{en:"Immediately call process.exit(0) without waiting",hi:"Bina intezar kiye turant process.exit(0) call karna"},{en:"Stop accepting new HTTP connections via server.close(), wait for in-flight requests to finish, close database/Redis pools cleanly, and then exit the process",hi:"server.close() se nayi requests rokna, chalti hui requests poori hone dena, database aur Redis pools safely close karna, aur fir exit karna"},{en:"Delete all node_modules files",hi:"node_modules delete karna"},{en:"Throw an unhandled exception",hi:"Unhandled exception throw karna"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"Graceful shutdown prevents dropping active user connections mid-request and prevents database connection leaks or transaction corruption.",hi:"Graceful shutdown active users ki requests poori hone deta hai aur DB connections ko cleanly close karta hai taaki data corrupt na ho."}},{question:{en:"Why does Stripe webhook signature verification (stripe.webhooks.constructEvent) fail if global express.json() parses the request body first?",hi:"Agar global express.json() pehle run ho jaye, toh Stripe webhook signature verification kyun fail ho jata hai?"},options:[{en:"Because Stripe does not support JavaScript",hi:"Kyunki Stripe JS support nahi karta"},{en:"Cryptographic HMAC signature verification requires the exact unmodified raw byte buffer of the HTTP payload; express.json() parses it into an object, altering character encoding and whitespace",hi:"Cryptographic HMAC verification ke liye original raw byte buffer chahiye hota hai; express.json() JSON parse karke raw bytes ko object bana deta hai jisse hash match nahi hota"},{en:"Because express.json() converts numbers to strings",hi:"Kyunki express.json() numbers badal deta hai"},{en:"Because Stripe webhooks only accept XML",hi:"Kyunki Stripe sirf XML leta hai"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"Stripe signature validation hashes the raw payload stream. Even a single changed whitespace character produces a completely different SHA-256 hash.",hi:"Stripe signature verify karne ke liye raw bytes ka hash check karta hai. express.json() object parse karke bytes change kar deta hai."}},{question:{en:"Why is app.set('trust proxy', 1) mandatory when deploying an Express app behind a reverse proxy (like Nginx, Cloudflare, or Render)?",hi:"Nginx, Cloudflare ya Render ke peeche Express app deploy karte waqt app.set('trust proxy', 1) kyun zaroori hota hai?"},options:[{en:"To disable SSL certificate checks",hi:"SSL checks band karne ke liye"},{en:"Without it, req.ip reflects the internal reverse proxy IP rather than the client's actual public IP, causing rate limiters to block all global users under a single IP",hi:"Iske bina req.ip mein client ke badle proxy ka IP aata hai, jisse rate limiter saare global users ko ek hi IP samajh kar ban kar deta hai"},{en:"To make Express run in multi-threaded mode",hi:"Express ko multi-threaded banane ke liye"},{en:"It is an optional setting with no security or operational effect",hi:"Iska koi effect nahi hai"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"Reverse proxies forward client IPs in the X-Forwarded-For header. 'trust proxy' tells Express to trust the header and populate req.ip with the real client IP.",hi:"Reverse proxy real client IP ko X-Forwarded-For header mein bhejta hai. 'trust proxy' se Express us header ko read karke sahi client IP identify karta hai."}},{question:{en:"How do you detect which objects are leaking memory in a Node.js heap snapshot using Chrome DevTools?",hi:"Chrome DevTools mein Node.js heap snapshot use karke memory leak wale objects ko kaise pehchana jata hai?"},options:[{en:"By looking at the CPU temperature gauge",hi:"CPU temperature dekh kar"},{en:"Take two snapshots over time, use the 'Comparison' view, find constructor types with a consistently positive # Delta, and inspect their 'Retainers' tree to find what references hold them in memory",hi:"Do snapshots le kar 'Comparison' view mein # Delta dekhein aur 'Retainers' tree se check karein ki kaunsa reference unhe memory mein pakad kar baitha hai"},{en:"By counting console.log statements",hi:"console.log count karke"},{en:"By restarting the server",hi:"Server restart karke"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"The Delta column reveals objects that continue growing between snapshots. The Retainers tree identifies the root references (such as global event listeners or closures) preventing garbage collection.",hi:"Comparison view mein Delta count badhte hue objects ko dikhata hai aur Retainers tree root cause variable ko reveal karta hai."}},{question:{en:"What is the primary difference between CommonJS (require) and ES Modules (import)?",hi:"CommonJS (require) aur ES Modules (import) mein mukhya farq kya hai?"},options:[{en:"CommonJS is synchronous and evaluated dynamically at runtime; ES Modules are asynchronous, statically analyzable at build time, and support top-level await and tree-shaking",hi:"CommonJS synchronous hota hai aur runtime par dynamically load hota hai; ES Modules asynchronous hote hain, build time par statically analyze hote hain, aur tree-shaking support karte hain"},{en:"CommonJS only runs in the browser; ES Modules only run in Node",hi:"CommonJS sirf browser mein chalta hai"},{en:"ES Modules cannot export functions",hi:"ES Modules functions export nahi kar sakte"},{en:"There is zero difference",hi:"Koi farq nahi hai"}],correctAnswer:0,explanation:{en:"CommonJS modules execute synchronously when required, allowing dynamic imports inside if conditions. ESM imports are hoisted and statically analyzed before execution.",hi:"CommonJS runtime par synchronous load hota hai jabki ESM static structure hone ki wajah se tree-shaking aur compile-time analysis permit karta hai."}},{question:{en:"Why does Node's EventEmitter print a 'MaxListenersExceededWarning' when more than 10 listeners are attached to an event by default?",hi:"Node ka EventEmitter by default 10 se zyada listeners judne par 'MaxListenersExceededWarning' kyun deta hai?"},options:[{en:"Because Node only has 10 memory addresses",hi:"Kyunki Node mein 10 addresses hote hain"},{en:"It acts as a defensive guard to alert developers to potential memory leaks where listeners are being repeatedly registered without being cleaned up",hi:"Ye memory leaks ko detect karne ka warning guard hai jab code baar-baar listeners attach karta hai bina purane listeners remove kiye"},{en:"Because events can only be fired 10 times total",hi:"Kyunki event sirf 10 baar fire ho sakta hai"},{en:"It is an unhandled syntax error",hi:"Ye syntax error hai"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"Attaching listeners in loops or per-request handlers without unregistering them causes memory leaks. The 10-listener threshold warns developers to verify their listener lifecycle.",hi:"Agar har HTTP request par naya event listener judta jaye aur delete na ho, toh memory bhar jaati hai. 10 ka limit is leak ki warning deta hai."}},{question:{en:"Why is Buffer.alloc(size) preferred over Buffer.allocUnsafe(size) for security-sensitive applications?",hi:"Security-sensitive apps mein Buffer.alloc(size) ko Buffer.allocUnsafe(size) ke muqable kyun prefer kiya jata hai?"},options:[{en:"Buffer.allocUnsafe only works on Windows",hi:"Buffer.allocUnsafe sirf Windows par chalta hai"},{en:"Buffer.alloc initializes the allocated memory with zeros; Buffer.allocUnsafe leaves old uninitialized data in RAM, potentially leaking sensitive passwords, keys, or previous memory contents",hi:"Buffer.alloc memory ko zeros se initialize karta hai; Buffer.allocUnsafe purana uninitialized RAM data waise hi chhod deta hai, jisse sensitive passwords ya keys leak ho sakti hain"},{en:"Buffer.alloc is 100x faster than allocUnsafe",hi:"Buffer.alloc 100x fast hai"},{en:"allocUnsafe throws an error on every call",hi:"allocUnsafe error throw karta hai"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"allocUnsafe is faster because it does not overwrite memory with zeros, but its buffer might expose previously allocated sensitive system memory to the network.",hi:"allocUnsafe memory ko zero nahi karta, jisse us RAM block mein bacha purana sensitive data (passwords, tokens) expose ho sakta hai."}},{question:{en:"How does V8's Generational Garbage Collector distinguish between the 'Young Generation' and 'Old Generation' in Node.js?",hi:"V8 ka Generational Garbage Collector 'Young Generation' aur 'Old Generation' ke beech kaise bhedbhav karta hai?"},options:[{en:"By looking at file creation dates on the disk",hi:"Disk par file dates dekh kar"},{en:"Short-lived objects start in the Young Generation (scavenged frequently); objects that survive multiple GC cycles are promoted to the Old Generation (swept and compacted infrequently)",hi:"Naye objects Young Generation mein allocate hote hain (frequent fast GC); jo objects multiple cycles survive karte hain wo Old Generation mein promote hote hain"},{en:"Old Generation is only for strings; Young Generation is only for numbers",hi:"Old sirf strings ke liye hai"},{en:"Generational GC was removed in V8 v8.0",hi:"Generational GC hata diya gaya tha"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"The Weak Generational Hypothesis states that most objects die young. Minor GC cycles clean the young nursery rapidly; long-lived objects are promoted to Old space.",hi:"Zyadatar objects turant useless ho jaate hain isliye Young space mein fast Scavenge GC hota hai. Persistent objects Old space mein promote ho jaate hain."}},{question:{en:"In modern Node.js (v15+), what happens by default when an asynchronous Promise rejects without a .catch() handler?",hi:"Modern Node.js (v15+) mein agar koi Promise bina .catch() ke reject ho jaye toh by default kya hota hai?"},options:[{en:"The rejection is silently ignored and the process keeps running",hi:"Rejection silently ignore ho jata hai"},{en:"Node.js terminates the process immediately with a non-zero exit code (unhandledRejection triggers process exit)",hi:"Node.js process ko turant non-zero exit code ke saath terminate (crash) kar deta hai"},{en:"Node automatically converts the error into an HTTP 200 response",hi:"Node ise 200 response bana deta hai"},{en:"Node sends an email to the server admin",hi:"Node email bhejta hai"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"Historically, unhandled rejections printed a warning. Since Node 15+, unhandled rejections terminate the process with exit code 1 to prevent running in corrupted state.",hi:"Node 15+ se unhandled promise rejections par process direct exit code 1 ke saath crash ho jata hai taaki server corrupted state mein na chale."}},{question:{en:"Why is configuring express.json({ limit: '50mb' }) globally a serious security vulnerability for API endpoints?",hi:"API endpoints ke liye globally express.json({ limit: '50mb' }) lagana ek gambhir security vulnerability kyun hai?"},options:[{en:"Because 50mb is not a valid number in JavaScript",hi:"50mb valid number nahi hai"},{en:"An attacker can flood the server with concurrent 50MB JSON payloads, forcing the single-threaded V8 engine to parse massive strings into RAM simultaneously, leading to DoS via heap exhaustion",hi:"Attacker multiple concurrent 50MB JSON payloads bhej kar V8 heap memory ko exhaust kar sakta hai aur DoS attack se server ko crash kar sakta hai"},{en:"Because JSON only supports files under 1MB",hi:"JSON sirf 1MB support karta hai"},{en:"It forces the server to use HTTPS only",hi:"Ye HTTPS force karta hai"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"JSON parsing is synchronous and CPU/RAM intensive. Restrict JSON limits to 100kb-1mb and stream large payloads (e.g. file uploads) via multipart streaming libraries like Multer.",hi:"JSON parse karna synchronous CPU aur RAM leta hai. 50MB ki requests aane par V8 memory crash ho jaati hai. Hamesha 1MB limit rakhein."}},{question:{en:"What determines whether a browser sends a CORS preflight OPTIONS request before making the actual HTTP call?",hi:"Browser actual HTTP call se pehle CORS preflight OPTIONS request bhejega ya nahi, ye kis par depend karta hai?"},options:[{en:"If the user is using Chrome or Firefox",hi:"Chrome ya Firefox use karne par"},{en:"If the request uses non-simple HTTP methods (PUT, DELETE, PATCH) OR custom headers (e.g. Authorization) OR non-standard Content-Types (e.g. application/json)",hi:"Agar request non-simple HTTP methods (PUT/DELETE), custom headers (Authorization), ya application/json Content-Type use karti hai"},{en:"Only on requests made after 6:00 PM",hi:"Sirf shaam 6 baje ke baad"},{en:"All cross-origin requests always trigger preflight unconditionally",hi:"Har request bina kisi condition ke preflight karti hai"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"Simple requests (GET/HEAD/POST with text/plain, multipart/form-data, or x-www-form-urlencoded and standard headers) skip preflight. Adding custom headers or JSON forces an OPTIONS preflight.",hi:"Agar Authorization header ya application/json use ho ya PUT/DELETE methods hon, toh browser pehle OPTIONS preflight request bhejta hai."}},{question:{en:"What is the primary difference between Express res.send(), res.json(), and Node's native res.end()?",hi:"Express ke res.send(), res.json() aur Node ke native res.end() mein mukhya farq kya hai?"},options:[{en:"res.end() closes the stream directly without headers; res.send() auto-detects content type and adds ETags; res.json() explicitly formats data as JSON and sets application/json Content-Type",hi:"res.end() bina headers ke stream close karta hai; res.send() type detect karke ETags lagata hai; res.json() JSON format karke application/json Content-Type set karta hai"},{en:"res.json() is only supported in React",hi:"res.json() sirf React mein chalta hai"},{en:"res.send() cannot send text",hi:"res.send() text nahi bhej sakta"},{en:"They are exact duplicates with different names",hi:"Teeno bilkul same hain"}],correctAnswer:0,explanation:{en:"res.end is low-level. res.send provides type inspection, caching headers, and formatting. res.json explicitly runs JSON.stringify and sets application/json.",hi:"res.end low-level stream method hai. res.send automatic headers aur ETags set karta hai. res.json specifically application/json format karta hai."}},{question:{en:"In Node's child_process module, what is the primary capability provided by fork() that standard spawn() lacks?",hi:"child_process module mein fork() aisi kaunsi mukhya suvidha deta hai jo standard spawn() mein nahi hoti?"},options:[{en:"fork() allows executing Python scripts",hi:"fork() Python scripts chalata hai"},{en:"fork() is specialized for spawning Node.js instances with a built-in IPC (Inter-Process Communication) messaging channel between parent and child via process.send() and process.on('message')",hi:"fork() specifically Node.js instances spawn karta hai aur parent-child ke beech built-in IPC channel deta hai jisse process.send() se messages exchange ho sakte hain"},{en:"fork() does not consume any RAM",hi:"fork() RAM consume nahi karta"},{en:"spawn() is deprecated in modern Node.js",hi:"spawn() deprecate ho gaya hai"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"fork() is a wrapper around spawn() tailored for Node processes, creating an IPC channel so processes can pass JSON messages and socket handles back and forth.",hi:"fork() specifically Node processes ke liye bana hai aur built-in IPC messaging channel deta hai jisse process.send() se direct data pass kiya ja sake."}},{question:{en:"What is the core architectural purpose of Top 25 Node.js & Express Interview Questions & Model Answers in the modern full-stack MERN ecosystem?",hi:"Modern full-stack MERN ecosystem mein Top 25 Node.js & Express Interview Questions & Model Answers ka mukhya architectural maksad kya hota hai?"},options:[{en:"It manages physical computer cooling and power supply",hi:"Ye physical computer cooling aur power supply manage karta hai"},{en:"It provides structured, robust, and scalable patterns for handling top 25 node.js & express interview questions & model answers with high performance and maintainability",hi:"Ye high performance aur maintainability ke saath Top 25 Node.js & Express Interview Questions & Model Answers ko handle karne ka structured aur scalable pattern provide karta hai"},{en:"It completely replaces JavaScript with binary machine code in browser tabs",hi:"Ye browser tabs mein JavaScript ko binary machine code se replace karta hai"},{en:"It is only used for printing documents to physical printers",hi:"Ye sirf physical printers par documents print karne ke liye hai"}],correctAnswer:1,explanation:{en:"Top 25 Node.js & Express Interview Questions & Model Answers establishes clean separation of concerns, ensuring predictable data flow, robust error handling, and optimal runtime performance in production.",hi:"Top 25 Node.js & Express Interview Questions & Model Answers clean architecture aur separation of concerns deta hai jisse application scalable aur maintainable banti hai."}}]};export{e as default};
